post_install() {
    cat << EOF

==> lobo is installed. Check the build it names:

    lobo -v

    A release build prints "lobo/0.1.5 (built with wolf <v>, pin <sha>)".
    A "+dev" suffix means the binary was not built at its release tag.
    Like nginx, lobo writes -v to STDERR -- redirect if you pipe it.

lobo takes a prefix (\`-p\`) the way nginx does, and the stock config's
paths are relative to it. /usr/share/lobo is a read-only template;
make a working copy and run from it:

    mkdir -p ~/lobo && cd ~/lobo
    cp -R /usr/share/lobo/conf /usr/share/lobo/html .
    mkdir -p logs
    lobo -t -c conf/lobo.conf        # test the config: syntax AND routing
    lobo -c conf/lobo.conf serve     # serve on 127.0.0.1:8080, foreground
    curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/   # the page
    lobo -s stop -c conf/lobo.conf   # stop; -s reload reloads

\`serve\` is a VERB, not a flag. nginx's \`-g "daemon off;"\` is accepted
by lobo's option parser but does NOT start the server -- you get the
banner and a refused connection.

There is no systemd unit and no /etc/lobo: 0.1.5 has a prefix, not a
service layout, and inventing one here would put the config where the
stock \`root html;\` cannot resolve.

Documentation:
--------------
    /usr/share/doc/lobo-bin/GETTING-STARTED.md
    /usr/share/doc/lobo-bin/docs/directives.md   every directive's status
    /usr/share/doc/lobo-bin/BUILD                toolchain, commit, pins

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat << EOF

==> lobo has been upgraded. Confirm the build (it prints to stderr):

    lobo -v

EOF
    # 0.1.5's one upgrade note an operator must act on, said once, on the
    # upgrade that crosses it ($2 is the version being replaced)
    if [ "$(vercmp "$2" 0.1.5)" -lt 0 ]; then
        cat << EOF
==> From 0.1.5, lobo started as root with no \`user\` line runs its workers
    as nobody (nginx's default) and its master keeps root: a file only root
    may read now answers 403. To keep 0.1.4's posture, add \`user root;\`.
    A running lobo is still the old binary: restart it now (lobo -s stop,
    then start it). Until you do, a worker its master replaces is started
    from the new /usr/bin/lobo beside old ones. Details:
    /usr/share/doc/lobo-bin/CHANGELOG.md, "Upgrading from 0.1.4".

EOF
    fi
}
